     Eternal City seems to have been, like many major, modern cities, a victim
of urban sprawl.  Nolonger confined to just the city itself, there are, or have
been in the recent past, 8 linear exits from EC to other cabals, and over a
dozen trans objects, both moving and stationary.
 
--> Currently there is no logic to many of the cabal entry objects cluttering
the Eternal City, even given our altered state of reality.
 
     -  Two castles within 30 rooms of each other with a city in between.
        [Castles Drakmoor and Doom].
     -  Take another few steps and you can be suddenly walking in a forest,
        or on the grassy planes of Africa, or around a tranquil lake.
     -  Although EC is a bustling, ever-changing metropolis, a WARP 
        Transphere [Vagabond entry obj] has been sitting in a pile of rubble
        for almost two years on Limbo Lane.
     -  Why is the X-men's Blackbird just sitting in a room south of heart ?
 
** Result ?  The Eternal City seems to have lost its cohesiveness as 
the centrepoint of EotL, as the place where past, present, future and
fantasy mesh and colide.
                                      
--> Currently there is no real motive for players to explore the mud.
 
     -  Nearly every cabal can be walked to within about 15 rooms of
        Heart, through either linear exits or trans objects handily scattered
        around EC.
     -  These same exits and objects also return the player to the 
        Eternal Cabal when their playing in that cabal is done.
     -  Given these, there is no real need for a player to explore the
        mud anymore, especially considering the simple layouts of 
        many areas.
 
** Result ?  Players just don't get out and adventure anymore, and thus
have gotten lazy.  Just think about the number of tells you get a day from
players who have gotten themselves lost because they didn't bother mapping 
where they were going.  Intellectual game play has decreased at a rapid rate.
 
>>  Our proposed solution:
1.  Rationalize cabal entry objects.
2.  Return a sense of containment to the Eternal City whilst encouraging
     players to adventure and explore beyond its limits.
 
--> Now picture this if you will..  Eternal City is actually up upon a plateau
in the rough geographical centre of an island.
     -  The sheer sides of the plateau mean that only four or five paths can
        wind their way down from the city.
     -  The paths lead to areas spread about the island; to a forest, an urban
        area, a desert.  
     -  They also lead to areas that link cabals in more reasonable forms;
        to a wharf, to long bridges stretching out across the sea, to a 
        transdimensional airport.  In other words, these areas use entry
        objects that are explainable and give the player a continued feel 
        that they can navigate freely throughout the "world" that is EotL.
     -  Our aim is not to remove linear exits and trans objects from the 
        Eternal City but to rationalize their existance.
 
     EotL is prehaps the only mud upon which players can wear a cloth tabard
and a chain mail coif whilst wielding a ThumpGun.  These inconsistancies are
part of what gives EotL its charm and makes it unique amongst alternative
worlds.  We are not trying to force the mud to standardize its objects or its
areas, indeed, it is more the opposite.  The wonderful diversity of the mud
can truly be appreciated when it exists in a more cohesive manner. 
